r Europe, is a continent characterized by a variety of health-related issues and dietary habits. It has a notable prevalence of celiac disease, with an incidence ranging from 1 in 85 to 1 in 540 individuals. The continent's dietary patterns reveal that saturated fat consumption primarily comes from meats and dairy products, which contributes to mortality rates associated with cardiovascular diseases (ECV). \n\nIn terms of nutritional health, Europe has seen an increase in the consumption of vitamin B supplements, particularly among individuals facing cognitive challenges. Additionally, it is estimated that between 10,000 and 15,000 people with short bowel syndrome in Europe rely on parenteral nutrition for more than 90 days. The region also has established guidelines for the refeeding of malnourished patients, recommending a caloric intake of 10-15 kcal/kg.\n\nRegulatory frameworks concerning nutritional information for food products are set and enforced across Europe, although there is significant variation in regulations regarding nutritional education and dietary (NED) practices among different countries. The continent also faces health challenges such as cystic fibrosis, with an incidence of 1 in every 3,500 births and a mean prevalence of 0.8 per 10,000 inhabitants. Healthcare spending on diabetes is a major concern, with a substantial portion of health budgets dedicated to managing this condition. Furthermore, Tolvaptán is available in Europe for the treatment of SIADH-related hyponatremia, and Southern Europe has a higher frequency of the IVS10 mutation linked to phenylketonuria.\",11\r\n4993,SEH-LELHA,\"SEH-LELHA is an organization dedicated to promoting health and wellness through various initiatives. It offers a food table and nutritional composition calculator as part of its comprehensive nutrition program, which aids individuals in understanding their dietary intake. Additionally, SEH-LELHA provides guidelines for physical exercise prescription specifica